STEP 1 -- what "scripting" is on this platform                                  PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method: read the CLI surface with no carriage return, so nothing is executed. The
  helper types `<prefix> ?`, then clears the line with Ctrl-U.

awplus# show script
  ^
  % Invalid input detected at '^' marker.

  No `show script` CLI command exists on this platform. The device answers with the
  caret and `% Invalid input detected at '^' marker`. A script file is listed with
  `dir flash:` and its contents are read in a `start-shell`.

awplus# activate ?
  SCRIPT      Script to activate
  background  Activate script in background
awplus# activate flash ?
  ARGUMENT  Arguments for the script
  <cr>

awplus(config)# trigger 1
awplus(config-trigger)# type ?
  atmf  cpu  env-sensor  interface  linkmon-probe  log  main-pse  memory  periodic
  ping-poll  pse-port  reboot  stack  time  usb
awplus(config-trigger)# type interface port1.0.20 ?
  any   Activate when any interface link status event occurs
  down  Activate when the interface closes
  up    Activate when the interface becomes operational
  <cr>
awplus(config-trigger)# type log ?
  LINE  Log filter regular expression
awplus(config-trigger)# script ?
  <1-5>  The list position of the script
awplus(config-trigger)# script 1 ?
  FILE  The file containing the script

  Entering `trigger 1` and leaving it without setting a `type` creates nothing. That was
  confirmed afterwards: `show trigger` still printed `% No triggers currently defined`.

  => STEP 1 PASS: scripting on this platform is a `.scp` file of ordinary AlliedWare Plus
  CLI commands held on the device's flash. It is run by the exec command
  `activate <file>`, or automatically by a `trigger`, which takes up to five script files
  in an ordered list. There is no scripting language, and there is no `show script`
  command.

  Why `type log` was chosen for STEP 4: every candidate port on this unit is an SFP cage
  with no module, so an interface event cannot be produced without disturbing a port that
  is carrying a link. `type log <regex>` needs no cable, because the DUT logs every CLI
  command it is given.

