STEP 4 -- a trigger runs a script, with a near-miss control                     PASS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
awplus# configure terminal
awplus(config)# trigger 1
awplus(config-trigger)# type log CK[0-9]33403FIRE
awplus(config-trigger)# script 1 flash:/ckt3b.scp
awplus(config-trigger)# end

  The regular expression is written so that it cannot match its own configuration line.
  The DUT logs every command typed, so a pattern that matched its own `type log` line
  would fire the trigger at the moment it was configured, and every later result would be
  ambiguous. `CK[0-9]33403FIRE` needs a digit after "CK". The configuration line contains
  the literal text `CK[0-9]33403FIRE`, whose next character is "[", which is not a digit.

awplus# show trigger 1
  Trigger Configuration Details
  ------------------------------------------------------------
  Trigger ..................... 1
  Description ................. <no description>
  Type and details ............ Log (CK[0-9]33403FIRE)
  Days ........................ smtwtfs
  After ....................... 00:00:00
  Before ...................... 23:59:59
  Active ...................... Yes
  Test ........................ No
  Trap ........................ Yes
  Repeat ...................... Continuous
  Modified .................... Thu Oct 22 01:08:37 2026
  Number of activations ....... 0            <-- it did not fire on its own config line
  Last activation ............. not activated
  Number of scripts ........... 1
    1. flash:/ckt3b.scp
    2. <not configured>
    3. <not configured>
    4. <not configured>
    5. <not configured>
  ------------------------------------------------------------
awplus# show running-config interface port1.0.21   -> still bare, no description

  The control first. "X" is not a digit, so this string must be rejected:
awplus# show clock | include CKX33403FIRE
  (read 8 s later)
awplus# show trigger 1
  Number of activations ....... 0
  Last activation ............. not activated
awplus# show running-config interface port1.0.21   -> still bare

  A log message was certainly produced, because the DUT logs every command. It differs
  from a matching string by one character. The trigger did not fire and the script did
  not run.

  Now the matching string. "7" is a digit:
awplus# show clock | include CK733403FIRE
  (read 10 s later)
awplus# show trigger 1
  Number of activations ....... 1            <-- the counter moved from 0 to 1
awplus# show running-config interface port1.0.21
  !
  interface port1.0.21
   description CKT33403-TRIGGER-RAN           <-- the second script's artefact
   switchport
   switchport mode access
  !
awplus# show running-config interface port1.0.22   -> still bare (control)

awplus# show log | include TRIGGER
  2026 Oct 22 01:09:23 user.notice awplus TRIGGER[4679]: Trigger 1 activated
  2026 Oct 22 01:09:23 user.notice awplus IMISH[4689]: [SCRIPT]description CKT33403-TRIGGER-RAN

  => STEP 4 PASS: the trigger ran the script with nobody typing the script's commands.
  The device's own log proves where the command came from: a command typed on a console
  is logged as `[manager@ttyS0]<command>`, and this one is logged as `[SCRIPT]<command>`.
  The activation and the script ran in the same second as the stimulus, 01:09:23. The
  condition is a real regular expression, because a string that differs by one character
  was rejected in the same run as the string that was accepted.

